===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|seneschal|lang=enm}} (recorded in English since 1393), from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|seneschal|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|ML.|en}} (Frankish) {{term|siniscalcus|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*siniz||senior}} + {{m|gem-pro|*skalkaz||servant}}; latter term as in {{term|marshal|lang=en}}. As an officer of the French crown, via {{etyl|fr}} {{term|sénéchal|lang=fr}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[steward]], ''[[particularly]]'' {{context|historical}} one [[in charge]] of a [[medieval]] [[nobleman]]'s [[estate]].
#* '''1884''', Mark Twain, ''Adventures of Huckleberry Finn '', Chapter 35
#*: ...so the very keenest '''seneskal''' can't see no sign...
# {{context|historical}} An [[officer]] of the [[crown]] in [[late]] [[medieval]] and [[early modern]] [[France]] who [[serve]]d as a [[kind]] of [[governor]] and [[chief]] [[justice]] of the [[royal]] [[court]] in [[Normandy]] and [[Languedoc]].

